BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Astrophysics and Cosmology] A Birefringence-Blind Consistency Tes
 t of the Planck Cosmic Birefringence Hint — Anto I. Lonappan (UCSD)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260928T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260928T130000
DTSTAMP:20260925T070000Z
UID:2793@physics.ucsd.edu
DESCRIPTION:<b>Speaker:</b> Anto I. Lonappan (UCSD)<br><br><b>Abstract:</b
 ><br>Several analyses of Planck\, ACT\, and WMAP data report a small unifo
 rm rotation of CMB polarization\, β ≈ 0.3–0.4°. If real\, this would
  be evidence of parity-violating physics such as axion-like fields coupled
  to photons. At this level\, however\, the signal is exactly degenerate wi
 th an overall miscalibration of the detectors' polarization angles. The le
 ading method\, the Minami–Komatsu (MK) likelihood\, breaks this degenera
 cy by using Galactic dust as a calibrator. That makes its detector angles
 \, and therefore β\, dependent on how the dust EB correlation is modeled.
 <br><br>In this talk\, I will present a complementary test that never uses
  that foreground model. For any pair of maps\, the cosmic rotation cancels
  exactly\, leaving only the relative instrumental angle. We measure these 
 relative angles from antisymmetric and symmetric cross-spectra. We then co
 mbine all pairs into a gauge-fixed calibration network\, with maps as node
 s and pairwise angles as edges\, to reconstruct each detector's calibratio
 n offset.<br><br>Applied to eight Planck NPIPE detector-set maps\, the net
 work reconstruction agrees with the MK calibration pattern (χ² = 8.18 fo
 r 7 degrees of freedom\, PTE = 0.32)\, with no map deviating by more than 
 1.8σ. I will explain what this does and does not tell us. Because both me
 thods use the same maps\, this is an independent method rather than indepe
 ndent data. It supports the calibration foundation of the β hint but cann
 ot fix its absolute zero point.<br><br>I will close by discussing how a su
 b-0.1° external calibration anchor\, combined with this network\, could d
 eliver an absolute birefringence measurement. This will be possible with B
 ICEP/Keck\, SPT-3G\, the Simons Observatory\, and LiteBIRD.<br><br><b>Abou
 t the Astro/Cosmology Seminar series:</b><br>The Center for Dark Cosmology
  and Gravitation seminar series (aka\, the Department of Physics Astrophys
 ics and Cosmology Seminar) will showcase key developments in astrophysics 
 and cosmology\, the implications of these for fundamental physics\, and vi
 ce versa\, the implications of laboratory particle physics discoveries for
  our understanding of the cosmos. Among the outstanding issues in physics
 \, the nature of the dark matter and dark energy and the origin of the sup
 ermassive black holes very early in the history of the universe stand out 
 as venues where beyond standard model physics is at play or likely plays a
  role. The pace of discovery in this field is breathtaking. The advent of 
 gravitational wave observatories\, the James Webb Space Telescope\, and th
 e Simons Observatory for the cosmic microwave background all complement a 
 host of laboratory experiments including LHC\, short and long baseline neu
 trino oscillation experiments\, neutrino less double beta decay searches\,
  and direct dark matter detection experiments\, to name just a few. Moreov
 er\, all of these research thrusts are underpinned by rapid development of
  computational capabilities and artificial intelligence. The CDCG is dedic
 ated to facilitating and bridging laboratory\, observational\, theoretical
  and computational studies focussed on the cosmos.<br><br><a href="https:/
 /physics.ucsd.edu/events/seminars-colloquia">https://physics.ucsd.edu/even
 ts/seminars-colloquia</a>
LOCATION:Mayer Room 4322
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Condensed Matter Seminar] Tuning Quantum Materials with Extreme P
 ressure and Magnetic Field — Yuhang Deng (Dept. of Physics\, UCSD)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261007T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261007T170000
DTSTAMP:20260930T070000Z
UID:2772@physics.ucsd.edu
DESCRIPTION:<b>Speaker:</b> Yuhang Deng (Dept. of Physics\, UCSD)<br><br>A
 bstract: Correlated electron materials based on lanthanides\, actinides\, 
 and transition metals host a rich landscape of emergent phenomena\, includ
 ing unconventional superconductivity\, exotic magnetism\, heavy-fermion be
 havior\, and nontrivial topological states. These phenomena emerge from th
 e interplay among strong electronic correlations\, spin–orbit coupling\,
  and competing energy scales\, and can be dramatically modified by externa
 l parameters such as pressure\, magnetic field\, and chemical composition.
 <br><br>In this talk\, I will discuss our efforts to uncover and control q
 uantum phases in correlated materials under extreme conditions of high pre
 ssure and high magnetic fields. Using diamond anvil cells\, which enable e
 lectrical\, magnetic\, and optical measurements at high pressures\, togeth
 er with complementary high-field techniques\, we investigate how these tun
 ing parameters<br>reshape electronic\, magnetic\, and structural propertie
 s. I will highlight three examples: pressure-induced metallization and sup
 pression of a magnetically ordered conducting surface state in FeSi\; pres
 sure-driven structural and valence evolution\, and new superconductivity i
 n UTe 2 \; and the emergence and evolution of multiple superconducting sta
 tes in U 1-x Th x Te 2 under high magnetic fields. Together\, these studie
 s demonstrate how pressure\, magnetic field\, and chemical substitution ca
 n tune the delicate balance among competing interactions in correlated ele
 ctron systems\, providing new perspectives on the mechanisms underlying un
 conventional superconductivity\, magnetism\, and topological quantum state
 s.<br><br>Yuhang Deng received his Ph.D. in Physics from Washington Univer
 sity in St. Louis in 2019. He subsequently joined Prof. M. Brian Maple’s
  group at the University of California San Diego\, where he worked as a po
 stdoctoral scholar and is currently an Assistant Project Scientist. His re
 search focuses on the experimental study of quantum materials under extrem
 e conditions\, particularly superconductivity and magnetism at high pressu
 res and high magnetic fields. His broader research interests include topol
 ogical and Kondo insulators\, unconventional superconductors\, and the app
 lication of diamond nitrogen-vacancy (NV) centers to high-pressure physics
 .<br><br><a href="https://physics.ucsd.edu/events/seminars-colloquia">http
 s://physics.ucsd.edu/events/seminars-colloquia</a>
LOCATION:Mayer 4322
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Physics Colloquium] Modeling density variations in microtubule-ba
 sed two-dimensional active nematics — Kevin  Mitchell (UC Merced)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261008T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261008T170000
DTSTAMP:20261002T070000Z
UID:2816@physics.ucsd.edu
DESCRIPTION:<b>Speaker:</b> Kevin  Mitchell (UC Merced)<br><br>Abstract: A
  dense two-dimensional layer of aligned microtubules (MTs)\, powered by mo
 lecular motors\, is a canonical laboratory model of active materials and a
  synthetic analog of biological systems such as bacterial turbulence\, mit
 otic spindles\, and morphogenesis. This material exhibits nematic ordering
  and associated topological defects\, which display complex emergent dynam
 ics\, including the creation and annihilation of defects and the braiding 
 of defects around one another in a complicated chaotic dance. Despite its 
 prominent role in research\, the MT-based active nematic material lacks a 
 well established theoretical model that accurately captures the rich densi
 ty variations prominently seen in experiments—--density variations that 
 are\, in fact\, the experimental signature of the nematic structure itself
 . The MT-system is typically modeled using two fields: the Q-tensor (encod
 ing the order and orientation of the nematic phase) and the fluid velocity
 \; critically\, the microtubule density is assumed to be constant. This tr
 aditional model is adopted from classical Landau-de Gennes liquid crystal 
 theory. In this talk\, we present a fundamentally different approach to mo
 deling MT-based active nematics that explicitly incorporates density varia
 tions\, producing simulations that strongly resemble experimental videos\,
  including the characteristic striation patterns. It also reproduces impor
 tant behavior of the system confined to a circular well—behavior seen ex
 perimentally\, but not captured by current theory. In crafting our model\,
  we present an alternative to Landau-de Gennes theory for the creation and
  annihilation of topological defects that does not rely on the classic iso
 tropic-nematic phase transition.<br><br><a href="https://physics.ucsd.edu/
 events/seminars-colloquia">https://physics.ucsd.edu/events/seminars-colloq
 uia</a>
LOCATION:NSB Auditorium
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Condensed Matter Seminar] Planckian Dynamics in Quantum Matter 
 — Debanjan Chowdhury (Cornell University)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261014T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261014T170000
DTSTAMP:20261008T070000Z
UID:2836@physics.ucsd.edu
DESCRIPTION:<b>Speaker:</b> Debanjan Chowdhury (Cornell University)<br><br
 >Abstract:<br>A long-standing puzzle in strongly correlated quantum matter
  is the ubiquity of a Planckian timescale governing transport\, appearing 
 across disparate materials and largely independent of microscopic details.
  Despite extensive experimental evidence\, this phenomenon has proven noto
 riously difficult to capture within conventional theoretical frameworks. I
 n this colloquium\, I will first explain why achieving microscopic control
  over Planckian dynamics is so challenging. I will then discuss a concrete
  example of a correlated material in which Planckian behavior can be under
 stood from first principles. Finally\, I will survey recent progress towar
 d deriving rigorous Planckian bounds\, drawing on ideas from quantum infor
 mation\, many-body physics\, and nonequilibrium statistical mechanics. Tog
 ether\, these developments suggest that Planckian dynamics may arise from 
 fundamental constraints on thermalization and information flow in quantum 
 matter.<br><br><a href="https://physics.ucsd.edu/events/seminars-colloquia
 ">https://physics.ucsd.edu/events/seminars-colloquia</a>
LOCATION:Mayer Room 4322
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Physics Colloquium] Superconductivity and magnetism in crystallin
 e graphite allotropes — Andrea Young (UC Santa Barbara)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261105T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261105T170000
DTSTAMP:20261006T070000Z
UID:2823@physics.ucsd.edu
DESCRIPTION:<b>Speaker:</b> Andrea Young (UC Santa Barbara)<br><br>I will 
 describe the physics of graphite crystals with rhombohedral stacking\, whe
 re the competition between electron hopping within- and between- the graph
 ene planes leads to a flat electronic dispersion and the crystallinity lea
 ds to a near total absence of disorder. The high electronic density of sta
 tes and Berry curvature—-both of which can be tuned by a perpendicular e
 lectric field—lead to a dizzying variety of magnetic and superconducting
  states\, often within the same device\, while the high experimental repro
 ducibility enabled by the crystalline nature of these structurally simple 
 systems allows us to investigate a variety of correlated electron phenomen
 a. I will focus on our efforts to use scanning nanoscale magnetometry to p
 robe the nature and origins of magnetism and unconventional superconductiv
 ity\, as well as the fate of the correlated electron liquid at high temper
 atures.<br><br><a href="https://physics.ucsd.edu/events/seminars-colloquia
 ">https://physics.ucsd.edu/events/seminars-colloquia</a>
LOCATION:NSB Auditorium
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
